   "start_page": 417,
   "summary": "This report surveys Japanese domestic and foreign affairs during July 1941, prominently featuring Japan's military occupation of southern French Indochina and the subsequent retaliatory asset freezes by the United States, Great Britain, and the Netherlands East Indies. It details American protests regarding aerial bombings near the USS Tutuila at Chungking and restrictions on American nationals and property across Japanese-controlled East Asia. In domestic politics, it documents the resignation of the Second Konoye Cabinet to remove Foreign Minister Matsuoka and the formation of the Third Konoye Cabinet under Admiral Teijiro Toyoda. Economic discussions cover acute trade disruptions caused by Allied freezing orders, foreign exchange controls, and agricultural declines in domestic crops.",
   "title": "Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of July 1941 (Report No. 1677)",
   "to": "Department of State"
  },
  {
   "about": "Despatch No. 5812 transmitting a duplicate copy of Report No. 1677 on conditions in Japan during July 1941 from Ambassador Joseph C. Grew to the Secretary of State.",
